WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:13.500
In which case, may we then move into 2014/15:
the commissioning round update.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.260
Simon Stevens: I think, again, Malcolm there
are two parts to this.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:21.590
I am going to ask Barbara to give an update
on where the specialised and the CCG commissioning

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:22.590
process got to.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:23.590
Paul, on the money.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:28.619
I think the fact is that a lot of the good
work that was described in 13/14 has left

00:00:28.619 --> 00:00:34.579
pressures which we either inherit or have
intensified going into 14/15.

00:00:34.579 --> 00:00:40.410
The reality is: if NHS spending is basically
flat in real terms and if we see spending

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:45.790
on high end tertiary care going up by 5% or
6%, that means cuts elsewhere.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:50.960
That has been pre empting the investments
that we want to make in primary services,

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.750
community health services, mental health services
and other parts of the environment.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:01:03.260
We have obviously had to build all of the
13/14 specialised overspend into the run rate

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:04.260
going into 14/15.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:14.479
However, it is absolutely crucial that we
do not have a repeat of this level of spending

00:01:14.479 --> 00:01:17.020
pre emption on specialised as we go into 15/16.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.350
And we have a very substantial work programme
underway now to ensure that does not happen.

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:25.119
In order to do that, as I said in my written
report, Paul Watson is leading a piece of

00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:29.390
work urgently over the next three months to
put in place appropriate controls there.

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:34.390
We are going to have to look at the extent
to which the specialist commissioning definition

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:39.659
was extended in a very expansive way - in
my view an over expansive way.

00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:45.259
Going into 13/14 we are going to have to find
new ways of getting CCGs to work with us on

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:50.749
some of this so that we have that population
base, a set of shared judgements around trade

00:01:50.749 --> 00:01:54.330
offs and a variety of other things as well,
which we will talk about in the future.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:56.079
However, that is the context in which the
commissioning round is occurring.

00:01:56.079 --> 00:01:59.020
And that is in addition to the pressures that
both Barbara and I have talked about and which

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:04.609
we need to sort out, around elective care,
the better care fund and so on.

00:02:04.609 --> 00:02:07.150
Barbara, do you want to give a State of the
Union?

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:12.880
Barbara Hakin: I think this is another comment,
which follows on from Moira's comment.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:18.010
This report is the tip of the iceberg, given
the huge amount of work is going on across

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:21.730
the NHS at the moment to do the planning and
contracting round.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:25.330
That is work in our area teams, for their
direct commissioning functions.

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:31.220
There are also commissioners working in CCGs
that are working with the teams in providers

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:35.299
to make sure that the plans we have in place
are right, deliver the right services for

00:02:35.299 --> 00:02:41.070
patients, but also deliver value for money
for the taxpayer.

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:47.340
I think will mainly focus on 14/15.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:52.840
We have talked about a longer term view and
as Simon has said, through to the autumn we

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:58.400
will be looking at the strategic plans in
order to put ourselves in a sustainable position

00:02:58.400 --> 00:02:59.760
for the future.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:06.030
However, for 14/15 we need to look at the
aspects of the planning and contracting round

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:13.620
which make sure we get the right services
and can balance the books.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.090
An enormous amount of work has been going
on.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:20.019
It is a joint piece of work between commissioning
operations and the directors of finance all

00:03:20.019 --> 00:03:25.840
the way through NHS England's system and through
the CCGs.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:29.519
We are making really good progress in what
is an extremely complex environment.

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:35.110
I will remind everyone how the planning process
works, if I may.

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:41.480
The contracts which are signed between commissioners
and providers are key to ensuring that providers

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:45.620
can plan for the volume of activity that they
need to deliver for our patients.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:49.380
They are key for commissioners because we
can look at all the different elements of

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:50.439
activity that is needed.

00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:53.810
That may be in urgent care, with the ambulance
services.

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:59.930
It may be to deliver the IAPT services that
we want to see or the full range of elective

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:00.930
care.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:06.340
One of the biggest issues that is discussed
in the planning round is: are we, as commissioners,

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:11.180
commissioning the right volumes of elective
care so that our providers can then put their

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:12.340
plans in place?

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:16.320
However, in the final analysis, I do think
it is important to remember that we have a

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.989
payment by results system.

00:04:18.989 --> 00:04:23.539
These plans are fundamental and important,
particularly in elective care.

00:04:23.539 --> 00:04:29.410
Any deviation from the plans that have been
agreed by commissioners will result in conversations

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:31.220
as the year goes on.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:36.050
However, providers are absolutely entitled
to and will charge commissioners for every

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:41.280
episode of elective activity underpayment
by results that they need to undertake to

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.010
deliver services for patients in line with
the standards that have been set.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:48.360
Therefore, although the planning round is
really important, there is still considerable

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.790
risk as you go through the year if activity
arises.

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:59.460
For context, I think it is important to remember
that the CCGs across England are in the process

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:03.620
of trying to agree 393 contracts with our
providers.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:11.400
Our specialised commissioners and area teams
are attempting to agree 291 contracts with

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:12.400
providers.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:16.060
That is an awful lot of contracts to come
together to give us the national position

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:17.979
that we want to see.

00:05:17.979 --> 00:05:18.979
We are making good progress.

00:05:18.979 --> 00:05:20.259
It is always iterative.

00:05:20.259 --> 00:05:25.270
Every day that goes by at the moment, more
contracts are agreed with providers and more

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:30.500
plans are put in place in which people say,
‘Yes, that is the level that we can agree

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:31.830
is necessary.'

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:38.490
I apologise for the error in section 7 of
the Board paper, I do not know whether anyone

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:40.120
has spotted it.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:47.560
There are only three Midlands &amp; East CCGs
with contracts not signed.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:51.069
That makes the national position five as of
the 8th May.

00:05:51.069 --> 00:05:57.289
I think you can see that is a very small number
of contracts that have gone to arbitration,

00:05:57.289 --> 00:06:01.680
compared to the very significant numbers of
contracts that we have.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.599
Since that time, more and more that are in
the column, ‘Contracts not signed and not

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:09.180
agreed,' have become agreed.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:13.630
It is our expectation that the number of contracts
from CCGs going to arbitration will increase

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:14.990
by one or two only.

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:18.610
Again, I think it is important to recognise
that, out of all those contracts, we will

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:22.550
only have a handful going to arbitration from
CCGs.

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:24.560
That is really important.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:32.800
However, we do now need to ensure at a national
level that the contracts that have been put

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.860
in place will deliver all the things that
we have committed to deliver in the mandate.

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:41.479
Many of the things is the mandate are delivered
through CCGs.

00:06:41.479 --> 00:06:44.220
We ask them for their plans.

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:49.610
In many cases we find the CCG plans very ambitious
and going further than we could have expected.

00:06:49.610 --> 00:06:51.810
That is great for patients in some areas.

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:55.010
However, in other areas we need to challenge
that ambition because we think it is not right

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:59.520
for patients or does not support what we need
to support to deliver our commitment for the

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:00.520
nation as a whole.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:05.360
We are going round that loop again and we
will very much be focusing on mental health.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:11.180
We will make sure that is getting the right
focus, particularly on IAPT and dementia.

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:15.639
We will, of course, be focusing on the elective
activity because of what we've talked about

00:07:15.639 --> 00:07:21.270
a few moments ago: our concerns about the
18 week referral to treatment standard that

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:23.879
we need to meet.

00:07:23.879 --> 00:07:26.780
Getting that activity right is not only vitally
important for those services provisions, it

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:29.080
is vitally important for us.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:33.349
If that volume of activity does not prove
to be correct then we run into financial problems

00:07:33.349 --> 00:07:34.560
as the year goes on.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.770
We are, therefore, working very closely with
our CCGs to make sure that what they are commissioning

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:40.770
is realistic.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:43.229
There is not one size to fit all.

00:07:43.229 --> 00:07:46.410
All the local health economies have entirely
different circumstances.

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:52.169
This means that, without that local knowledge,
you cannot really assess whether the amount

00:07:52.169 --> 00:07:55.580
of activity commissioned is broadly in line
with what you expected.

00:07:55.580 --> 00:08:00.410
That assessment is what all our area teams
are doing.

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:04.930
We have made a very significant amount of
progress on specialised commissioning over

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:06.419
the last few weeks.

00:08:06.419 --> 00:08:08.310
It is an enormously challenging area.

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:12.319
I will not reiterate the reasons why specialised
commissioning has been even more challenging

00:08:12.319 --> 00:08:16.190
for NHS England since we came into being.

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:22.979
It will continue to be challenging because,
inevitably, these are patients who are very

00:08:22.979 --> 00:08:26.240
seriously ill and need very complex treatments.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:32.920
However, to reiterate what has already been
said: we all have the ambition to support

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:36.690
patients so they do not become ill in the
first place, to keep them well in the first

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:37.690
place.

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:41.430
To achieve that we absolutely need to make
sure that what is going on in specialised

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:45.140
is not compromising our ability to do what
we need to do.

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:57.240
We have put reserves into specialised commissioning
which area teams can use when going through

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:58.420
the planning round with providers.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:01.670
I can report that since the addition of these
reserves, for the most part, we have agreement

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:05.970
with providers across all of England on specialised
commissioning.

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:12.170
And that is within the allocation that the
commissioners have been given.

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:16.760
We will again see a small handful going to
arbitration.

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:21.630
Everyone has broadly closed the gap and can
agree with their local provider that this

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:24.170
is broadly the level of activity that we expect
to see.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:26.730
However, there are a couple of notable exceptions.

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:33.730
These are often very large hospitals in London,
which have a very high volume of specialised

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:34.810
activity.

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:39.120
The resolution of those contracts is really
holding up everything, because it is often

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:41.520
holding up the CCG contracts as well.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:47.050
However, I do think we in NHS England have
a duty to make sure these contracts are pulled

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:50.250
together in a way that does not compromise
those populations.

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:57.480
It is the same population that will require
specialised services and secondary healthcare

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:00.790
and primary care and preventative services.

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:05.660
So we are still continuing to work with one
or two of those hospitals as to how we can

00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:10.750
manage that budget and the planning of those
services in a way that does not compromise

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:13.300
other important things that we need to see.

00:10:13.300 --> 00:10:18.380
We are optimistic that over the next week
or so that will, for the most part, all be

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:19.380
resolved.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:23.990
Again, that does not, in any shape or form,
underestimate the risk.

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:28.180
As I have discussed, I think there are risks
in the CCG contracts, particularly in terms

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:29.350
of activity.

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:35.900
For in a number of the contracts there is
an ambition, which is the right ambition,

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:40.110
to support patients not necessarily to be
admitted and to have alternative treatments.

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:45.610
If that does not come off, because of the
nature of PBR that puts the CCGs at risk.

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:51.089
I think there is a much greater degree of
risk in the specialised commissioning contracts

00:10:51.089 --> 00:10:56.730
because of the activity levels that have been
agreed with providers, but as Simon says,

00:10:56.730 --> 00:11:01.510
since April the first we have been allowed
to make a huge increase in the capacity we

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:06.200
have nationally to over see the specialised
commissioning budgets and I would really like

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:11.940
to commend Paul, the turnaround and all the
others in all the other bits of NHS England.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:17.089
I would particularly like to commend the area
teams doing the contracting, who have got

00:11:17.089 --> 00:11:21.519
us into a place whereby we can get through
the planning round, close the contracts and

00:11:21.519 --> 00:11:28.829
move forward into treating patients in a way
that makes sure that we get the best value

00:11:28.829 --> 00:11:31.430
for the money that we have.

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:34.470
Paul Baumann: It is difficult to add much
to that.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:37.540
Therefore I probably will not, except to outline
the shape of the numbers that emerge from

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:38.540
that.

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:42.040
If you remember back to the allocations paper,
we had a very clear strategy about how the

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:46.290
draw down in 14/15 and 15/16 was to be deployed.

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:48.330
We have £400 million of draw down.

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:54.079
That is just surplus from previous applied
in year to financial needs of one sort or

00:11:54.079 --> 00:11:55.079
another.

00:11:55.079 --> 00:11:57.790
In 14/15 we said priority would be given to
specialised commissioning.

00:11:57.790 --> 00:12:01.910
It is clear why that is necessary from all
the discussion this morning.

00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:05.519
That is the reserve that Barbara was talking
about, which we have now allocated to the

00:12:05.519 --> 00:12:07.649
relevant area teams.

00:12:07.649 --> 00:12:11.370
The consequence of that is, of course, that
every other part of the system needs to work

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:14.519
within the allocation for the year.

00:12:14.519 --> 00:12:20.290
We are now very close to getting the plans
themselves to balance to that expectation.

00:12:20.290 --> 00:12:24.440
We are about £200 million off, in total,
as of the end of last week.

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:27.610
That is roughly half and half, CCGs and direct
commissioning.

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:32.570
As a reminder, £200 million sounds like a
lot of money; it is a fraction of a percentage

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:35.519
of the resource that we have.

00:12:35.519 --> 00:12:39.120
On the CCG front, clearly the challenge is
to make sure that the aggregate of all the

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:41.970
CCGs added together do not have a draw down.

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.610
That does not have to mean that every single
one cannot have a draw down.

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:46.910
It means the totality cannot.

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:49.570
We are about £100 million short of that at
the moment.

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:55.190
We are working very intensively with the commissioning
assembly working group for finance, with whom

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:58.730
we did the work on the allocations and the
planning process last year.

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:02.300
We have now brought together to tackle the
rather more immediate challenges.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:06.610
Indeed, we will continue that as the year
goes by, because I think the need will arise.

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:11.589
So we are working with them on this issue
of getting the CCG draw down to a neutral

00:13:11.589 --> 00:13:18.420
position, ideally by people adopting a strategy
of, essentially, saving their draw down for

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:19.420
the years it was planned.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:22.899
It was planned to come in future years rather
than this year.

00:13:22.899 --> 00:13:27.590
I will report again on progress with that
in the near future.

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:31.260
On the direct commissioning side, we are very
close, I think, to being ready on primary

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:32.260
care.

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:36.680
That is ready in terms of being in balance
in total over that, having absorbed some quite

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:39.670
specific pressures in primary care.

00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:41.760
Of course, the big challenge is specialised
commissioning.

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:43.870
However, I think enough has been said about
that.

00:13:43.870 --> 00:13:47.180
And the biggest challenge, of course, is now
getting the plans down to zero.

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:51.790
However, I am confident we will do that in
the next few days.

00:13:51.790 --> 00:13:57.430
The big challenge is then de risking the discontinuity
there needs to be between the trends of the

00:13:57.430 --> 00:14:00.089
past and where that takes us to for the future.

00:14:00.089 --> 00:14:02.600
Malcolm Grant: Thank you Paul.

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:09.710
It is going to be an extremely difficult year
and the year after will possibly be even worse.

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:15.470
I think we all commend the work that is being
done to ensure that the contracts are in place

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:17.389
and the planning is place.

00:14:17.389 --> 00:14:22.680
We are conscious of the way in which activity
can continue to drive cost through the system.

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:25.790
And I know that the Board will wish to have
regular updates on how we are managing the

00:14:25.790 --> 00:14:26.790
risk.

00:14:26.790 --> 00:14:30.110
Particularly, of course, the risks in specialised
commissioning, in the light of what both Simon

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:33.440
and Barbara have spoken to us about this morning.

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:35.660
Can I invite further comments from members
of the Board?

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:36.660
Ciarán?

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:38.089
Ciarán Devane: It is just on the risk thing.

00:14:38.089 --> 00:14:40.860
This perhaps picks up on your comment, Malcolm.

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:46.110
It is depressing enough if you take a one
year view; if you take a three, four or five

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:48.170
year view, it is all cumulative.

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:51.410
And it is not only the financial risk.

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:54.370
For me, this is existential.

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:58.860
If we want the long term sustainable NHS with
more investment in primary care, community

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:03.589
and mental health as Simon was describing,
there are an awful lot of things that have

00:15:03.589 --> 00:15:05.240
to come right.

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:07.040
We have to control specialised commissioning.

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:09.940
All the CCGs, on average, have to balance.

00:15:09.940 --> 00:15:17.550
It is just whether we feel there is enough
IQ and capacity in the system to do the thinking

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:20.800
to make some of the changes to allow the strategy
bit of this.

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:22.670
That is, the strategy around the six characteristics.

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:29.570
The changes will allow us to do enough of
that to keep people out of hospital, to help

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:33.930
people get out of hospital, to link in to
social care.

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:40.870
It is whether the strategy bit is in place,
as well as the control in specialised commissioning.

00:15:40.870 --> 00:15:43.760
It gets back to this pooling of budgets.

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:49.000
We have to get that right in an awful lot
of places very quickly if all of this is going

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:50.000
to work.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:54.540
Simon Stevens: I agree with that analysis
completely.

00:15:54.540 --> 00:16:04.019
I think we have to avoid a false choice between
assuming that 211 micro strategies can somehow

00:16:04.019 --> 00:16:09.950
sort this out or, to the extent there is risk
in that, assuming therefore just one big single

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:11.389
approach across the country.

00:16:11.389 --> 00:16:14.980
Therefore, what we are attempting to do now
is on the back of work that has been done

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:19.920
by CCGs, which is supposed to be complete
by 20th June for their five year plans and

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:23.360
on the back of work done by hospitals, which
is supposed to be complete by 30th June for

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:24.569
their five year plans.

00:16:24.569 --> 00:16:31.079
We are looking at that and pivoting to a different
kind of strategy process that will bring together

00:16:31.079 --> 00:16:35.839
some of the national work with the particular
local circumstances that exist in different

00:16:35.839 --> 00:16:37.100
health communities around the country.

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:41.889
The aim is to say, ‘In this particular geography,
what is it you would have to do in order to

00:16:41.889 --> 00:16:44.649
put yourself on a sustainable footing?

00:16:44.649 --> 00:16:46.959
How do we ensure that?'

00:16:46.959 --> 00:16:53.720
Many of the new ways of working are the important
things that we were talking about: giving

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:56.600
more power to patients, more support for communities
themselves.

00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:59.640
They are actually driving a lot of the energy
of that conversation.

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:04.240
So there is a huge effort required on that.

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:09.130
The truth is, I expect the answer will be
that it is not going to be some kind of neat

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:13.770
circle that can be readily be squared when
you add it all up across the country for five

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:14.770
years.

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:17.070
That is the conversation we are going to be
having to have on the back of this, once we

00:17:17.070 --> 00:17:18.070
have the work.

00:17:18.070 --> 00:17:24.130
That is a conversation with the people on
England about what the health service is going

00:17:24.130 --> 00:17:28.440
to need to look like under alternative scenarios,
come the autumn.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:36.560
That said, let us also remind ourselves that
we are in year five of the most sustained

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:39.310
funding squeeze the National Health Service
has had since 1948.

00:17:39.310 --> 00:17:45.740
As everybody has said, it is a huge tribute
to the staff of the health service that, in

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:49.830
face, the quality of care remains incredibly
high in the vast majority of cases.

00:17:49.830 --> 00:17:55.320
That is, in large part, thanks to the effort
and the good will and personal sacrifice of

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:59.230
the 3 million people who are working for us.

00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:02.020
There is a lot to be proud of in where the
NHS has got to.

00:18:02.020 --> 00:18:04.360
However, as you say, Ciarán, there is an
awful lot of stakes.

00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:08.110
In fact, as I said on my first day, the stakes
have never been higher for the National Health

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:12.350
Service than they are now and over the next
five years.

00:18:12.350 --> 00:18:14.530
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:18.350
On that sobering note, may we turn to the
next paper?

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:25.620
That is the Board assurance framework for
next year and for the 2013/14 BAF also.

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:27.890
Do you want to introduce this, Simon?

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:28.910
Simon Stevens: Yes.

00:18:28.910 --> 00:18:33.200
I realise this is building on a lot of work
that has been done during the course of the

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:39.550
last year to bring a real forensic discipline
to thinking about the shape of risks and more

00:18:39.550 --> 00:18:42.300
importantly, what to then do about them across
the organisation.

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:47.660
I think there has been a lot of external support
for the approach that has been taken.

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:52.680
I know Ed has been instrumental in driving
this and provides that external perspective,

00:18:52.680 --> 00:18:54.720
so he may want to speak as well.

00:18:54.720 --> 00:19:00.420
What we are doing here now, I think, is a
refresh for 2014/15.

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:06.070
I think all the principle risks are appropriately
listed here.

00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:10.700
We are going to want to make sure we have
properly kicked the tyres on how they are

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:13.450
calibrated, for both unremediated and remediated.

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:15.580
That also goes for how they are clustered.

00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:20.440
Some of these are broad environmental risks
that every industrialised country is facing.

00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:25.750
These include the hopefully low possibility
but potentially very significant impact, were

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:32.000
it to occur, of pandemic flu through to pressure
which exist in the National Health Service

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:34.160
for which the commissioning system has responsibility.

00:19:34.160 --> 00:19:39.130
There are also some specific programmes that
we at NHS England need to get right.

00:19:39.130 --> 00:19:46.930
For example, handling public enquiries and
complaints or the work that we do around supporting

00:19:46.930 --> 00:19:50.010
CCGs with their out of hours 111 service and
so on.

00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:54.030
I think we can categorise and cluster in order
to manage.

00:19:54.030 --> 00:20:01.560
However, Ed, I think this represents a pretty
coherent way of thinking about the shape of

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:04.310
risk in what you have said.

00:20:04.310 --> 00:20:07.910
Ed Smith: Yes it does.

00:20:07.910 --> 00:20:08.940
I have several points.

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:11.870
One is that from the start we have been transparent
about this.

00:20:11.870 --> 00:20:17.310
It is great that our approach to risk is out
there.

00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:18.910
It is shared with DH.

00:20:18.910 --> 00:20:26.380
DH only yesterday took a note which recognised
the way in which we approached risk management

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:28.510
through the Board assurance framework.

00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:34.520
Our internal auditors are Deloitte, who are
very rigorous, very independent and challenging.

00:20:34.520 --> 00:20:48.460
They have commended our approach to risk management
in their internal audit opinion.

00:20:48.460 --> 00:20:54.080
Identifying gross risk is a management process
and a discussion process and one which is

00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:55.080
in train.

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:58.830
The chart I like best is the diagram that
shows you the mitigating actions that you

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:02.080
are taking to bring that gross risk down.

00:21:02.080 --> 00:21:07.930
The real power behind this is that management
process: discussing gross risk, discussing

00:21:07.930 --> 00:21:13.170
mitigation and then tracking to ensure the
mitigation actions are having effect.

00:21:13.170 --> 00:21:20.050
It is not the concept of risk management,
but rather the management processes that are

00:21:20.050 --> 00:21:21.290
taken from it.

00:21:21.290 --> 00:21:24.500
I think we take this extraordinarily seriously.

00:21:24.500 --> 00:21:26.290
We apply a lot of effort to it.

00:21:26.290 --> 00:21:34.400
However, Simon, I commend you to take it forward
in this way, of continued refinement, for

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:36.370
the future.

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:41.600
It is not as dry as it looks, I have to say.

00:21:41.600 --> 00:21:46.390
I know that might reflect on me being a bit
of a sad…

00:21:46.390 --> 00:21:49.850
But actually, it is an incredibly powerful
management tool.

00:21:49.850 --> 00:21:52.450
Barbara Hakin: Thank you for that.

00:21:52.450 --> 00:21:59.020
I present this paper on behalf of the risk
management committee, which I chair at executive

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:00.020
level.

00:22:00.020 --> 00:22:02.090
I think, for today, it is work in progress.

00:22:02.090 --> 00:22:05.780
They have come a long way, but there is more
to do on the particular issues.

00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:10.410
It seems to me it is so critical that this
document identifies the biggest risks clearly

00:22:10.410 --> 00:22:16.620
and plainly for the Board and the mitigations
we have in place, so the Board can take assurance

00:22:16.620 --> 00:22:17.620
on those.

00:22:17.620 --> 00:22:22.950
They can also then challenge when they do
not feel those assurances are adequate.

00:22:22.950 --> 00:22:27.720
However, given it is an iterative process,
there are two or three things that we need

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:28.720
to do today.

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:32.940
Firstly, we were challenged with producing
something simpler, more amenable, more user

00:22:32.940 --> 00:22:35.450
friendly, less dry, as Ed said.

00:22:35.450 --> 00:22:39.830
I hope all board members who have not been
as involved as the executive team do find

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:42.210
this an easy format to use.

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:44.800
Although, we will need to do more work on
the segment.

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:47.540
So, any comments on the format?

00:22:47.540 --> 00:22:51.660
I think it is important to note the closure
and the transition of the risks from the old

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:52.660
assurance framework.

00:22:52.660 --> 00:22:57.840
It is also important that the Board accepts
that risks that have been closed have been

00:22:57.840 --> 00:22:58.840
reflected in here.

00:22:58.840 --> 00:23:02.810
I think it is again important that we note
today the current high level risks.

00:23:02.810 --> 00:23:08.620
However, I would suggest that probably during
the course of the Board meeting and conversations

00:23:08.620 --> 00:23:14.320
we have shown that these risks are getting
significant attention.

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:21.150
Finally, any particular steer on the next
steps as we move forward on making this even

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:30.182
better than it is now?

00:23:30.182 --> 00:23:31.182
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:23:31.182 --> 00:23:32.182
Margaret?

00:23:32.182 --> 00:23:33.182
Margaret Casely Hayford: I normally really
enjoy words and I am terrified by depictions,

00:23:33.182 --> 00:23:34.470
such as the chart that Ed likes.

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:47.270
However, I have to say that I really do echo
Ed's point about the really helpful comparison

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:50.800
of the gross picture to the net picture.

00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:59.850
What it does is to build in a sort of forward
movement and optimism: this is where we are

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:01.770
going with our mitigating.

00:24:01.770 --> 00:24:07.800
From the perspective of a board member who
would normally receive an audit report and

00:24:07.800 --> 00:24:12.950
feel that sinking feeling in my stomach, I
think this is really enormously helpful and

00:24:12.950 --> 00:24:15.710
I thank all of those who have been involved
in it.

00:24:15.710 --> 00:24:16.710
Thank you.

00:24:16.710 --> 00:24:17.770
It is a good piece of work and a good tool.

00:24:17.770 --> 00:24:25.810
Malcolm Grant: Karen, do you want to say anything
about the next phase of development?

00:24:25.810 --> 00:24:30.480
Karen Wheeler: Obviously, I will be taking
it over from Barbara as this falls to me.

00:24:30.480 --> 00:24:33.540
I will want to build on the process that we
have.

00:24:33.540 --> 00:24:41.620
I take risk incredibly seriously and it is
really important that we continue to develop

00:24:41.620 --> 00:24:46.830
this as a management tool, so that we bring
the right information to you in a coherent

00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:47.830
way.

00:24:47.830 --> 00:24:50.970
However, it is also important that we actively
use it in our management process.

00:24:50.970 --> 00:24:56.850
The most important thing is that we are taking
the action on the risks, obviously.

00:24:56.850 --> 00:25:00.580
I do not quite know what it will look like
when it comes back.

00:25:00.580 --> 00:25:04.543
Hopefully you will see a trajectory and progression
on this.

00:25:04.543 --> 00:25:08.150
Malcolm Grant: With refinement along the lines
that the Chief Executive has mentioned?

00:25:08.150 --> 00:25:09.330
Karen Wheeler: Absolutely.

00:25:09.330 --> 00:25:11.530
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much.

00:25:11.530 --> 00:25:15.490
The paper is taken and approved.

00:25:15.490 --> 00:25:20.700
Malcolm Grant: May we please now move on to
item 9, which is the Board committee feedback.

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:23.770
We have reports from a number of committees
of the Board.

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:27.020
This is obviously not an opportunity to reopen
the deliberations of the committee.

00:25:27.020 --> 00:25:32.900
It is simply to learn, for our own education,
what has been going on.

